description:
The public contact form handler forwards the client-supplied `targets` multipart field verbatim as the outbound `to` recipient of the site's transactional email provider. No authentication, no session, no CAPTCHA, and no application-level rate limit protects it. The shipped front-end bundle proves the recipient is a *client* input and not server-derived: chunk `/_next/static/chunks/275-ea7b562607231006.js` contains

  let V = async () => { let l = new FormData; ... l.set("targets", a.targets);
  let D = await fetch("/api/send", { method:"POST", body:l }).then(l=>l.json());
  null !== D.data ? k("success") : k("fail") }

so `targets` is read straight off `settings.fields.targets` in the browser and posted to the API. The server does not pin it to the site owner.

The handler delegates to a Resend-style mail API and returns its response verbatim, which gives an unauthenticated attacker a direct, repeatable primitive to send authenticated-looking HTML/text email from the `infinitycapital.bh` corporate domain to ANY external recipient of the attacker's choosing. Returned `{"data":{"id":"01a0f1dd-d7fb-729c-86f6-c1d937b4f622"},"error":null}` is the provider accepting the message for delivery (not a validation failure).

Impact: spam/phishing relay on a trusted corporate sending domain (damages the brand's domain reputation and can get the domain blacklisted), plus a free bulk-mail primitive billed to the victim, and a quota-exhaustion DoS against the legitimate contact form — the only thing that stopped the abuse during testing was the provider's own monthly quota, which is itself attacker-spendable. The provider is only in test mode, which blocklists `example.com`; that blocklist is a provider-side anti-spam control, not an authorization control, and it demonstrably does NOT stop arbitrary non-example.com recipients.

The `/api/send` handler returns the upstream email provider's raw error object to an unauthenticated client. Because of this passthrough, an anonymous caller learns internal account state about the victim's mail provider account — specifically that the account is in a restricted/test mode and that a monthly sending quota exists and has been exhausted:

  {"data":null,"error":{"statusCode":429,"message":"You have reached your monthly email sending quota.","name":"monthly_quota_exceeded"}}

  {"data":null,"error":{"statusCode":422,"name":"validation_error","message":"Invalid `to` field. Please use our testing email address instead of domains like `example.com`. See our documentation for more information."}}

This discloses (a) the identity and behaviour of the transactional mail provider, (b) the account is in a test-mode plan that refuses `example.com` recipients, and (c) a live monthly quota counter with its current state. An attacker reads that to time the abuse of the companion open-relay issue exactly: fire until `monthly_quota_exceeded` appears, which both maximises spam sent and guarantees the legitimate contact form is dead for the remainder of the billing month (a low-cost, targeted DoS of the business's customer-inquiry channel). The quota state is also a usable oracle: it lets an attacker measure their own abuse rate and of other traffic hitting the shared account.

Additionally, the same handler returns HTTP 500 with an EMPTY body for several malformed inputs (empty body, `null`, `{"targets":12345}`, `application/xml` body, `text/plain` body, and a missing `targets` field), which is an unhandled-exception signature distinct from the handled 422 validation path — useful for fingerprinting the backend and indicating the provider integration lacks input normalisation.

proof_of_concept:
No cookies, no Authorization header, no CSRF token — anonymous request.

Step 1 — arbitrary-recipient send succeeds and returns a Resend message id:
$ curl -s -X POST "https://www.infinitycapital.bh/api/send" \
   -H "User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64) Firefox/128.0" \
   -F "fname=Vapt" -F "lname=Tester" -F "areacode=388" -F "tel=123456" \
   -F "cname=vaptuser" -F "subject=Test inquiry" -F "msg=Hello from pentest" \
   -F "check=1" -F "targets=vapt@www.infinitycapital.bh"
HTTP=200 {"data":{"id":"01a0f215-fdc2-7aca-84ad-31ee3b177093"},"error":null}

Step 2 — ~8 further anonymous sends exhaust the organization's MONTHLY quota:
HTTP=200 {"data":null,"error":{"statusCode":429,"name":"monthly_quota_exceeded",
        "message":"You have reached your monthly email sending quota."}}

Step 3 — reproduction after quota reset, three back-to-back anonymous sends each consuming quota and minting a new Resend id:
attempt1 HTTP=200 {"data":{"id":"01a0f218-032e-777e-a1ec-9a97fe725178"},"error":null}
attempt2 HTTP=200 {"data":{"id":"01a0f218-20bb-7686-b714-c405906174ef"},"error":null}
attempt3 HTTP=200 {"data":{"id":"01a0f218-80d4-7540-bd61-614bdb5524bd"},"error":null}
Control: GET /contact -> HTTP=200 (the site is reachable; only the mail quota is exhausted)

Client-side impact (from /_next/static chunk p10js/0543ad91.js):
  let D = await fetch("/api/send",{method:"POST",body:l}).then(l=>l.json());
  null!==D.data ? k("success") : k("fail")
=> every legitimate visitor is shown "fail" while the quota is exhausted.
